            <fr:route>/ffi/sec-foundations/</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="finite-field-invariants: a reference forest › Foundations"><fr:link href="/ffi/index/" title="finite-field-invariants: a reference forest" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/index/" display-uri="index" type="local">finite-field-invariants: a reference forest</fr:link> › Foundations</fr:title>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <html:p>From the conjugation action to the statement of the height
  conjecture.</html:p>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors />
                <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0001/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>ffi-0001</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/ffi/ffi-0001/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="Setup: conjugation over a finite field">Setup: conjugation over a finite field</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>definition</fr:taxon>
                <fr:meta name="area">foundations</fr:meta>
                <fr:meta name="provenance">distilled.tex Part 1; journal sec:setup.</fr:meta>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>Fix a prime power <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[q]]></fr:tex>, let <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[k = \mathbb {F}_q]]></fr:tex> and let <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[K = \overline {k}]]></fr:tex>.
Let <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[S = k[x_{ij} : 1 \le  i, j \le  n]]]></fr:tex> be the coordinate ring of
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathrm {M}_n = \mathrm {M}_{n \times  n}]]></fr:tex>, with generic matrix <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[X = (x_{ij})]]></fr:tex>,
carrying the conjugation action of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[G = \mathrm {GL}_n(k)]]></fr:tex>: the element
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\sigma  \in  G]]></fr:tex> maps the variables to the entries of
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\sigma ^{-1} X \sigma ]]></fr:tex>. Write <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathcal {N} \subset  \mathrm {M}_n(K)]]></fr:tex> for the
nilpotent cone.</html:p>
                <html:p>The kernel of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[G \to  \mathrm {GL}(\mathrm {M}_n(k))]]></fr:tex> is the scalars, so the
effective group is <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\widehat {G} \cong  \mathrm {PGL}_n(k)]]></fr:tex>. For <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n \ge  2]]></fr:tex> the
order of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\widehat {G}]]></fr:tex> is divisible by the characteristic of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[k]]></fr:tex>, so
the action is <html:em>modular</html:em>: there is no Reynolds operator, and the
classical non-modular invariant theory does not apply.</html:p>
                <html:p><html:strong>Role in the skeleton.</html:strong> This node fixes only the ambient objects and the group. The two
things the setup produces —
<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0054/" title="The characteristic polynomial coefficients" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0054/" display-uri="ffi-0054" type="local">the characteristic polynomial coefficients</fr:link> and
<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0055/" title="The entrywise Frobenius" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0055/" display-uri="ffi-0055" type="local">the entrywise Frobenius</fr:link> — are separate statements, because
almost every downstream node rests on one of them and not on the other.</html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors />
                <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0054/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>ffi-0054</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/ffi/ffi-0054/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="The characteristic polynomial coefficients">The characteristic polynomial coefficients</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>definition</fr:taxon>
                <fr:meta name="area">foundations</fr:meta>
                <fr:meta name="provenance">distilled.tex Part 1; journal sec:setup. The caution was added 2026-08-23, correcting a claim that <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[S^G]]></fr:tex> is generated by the <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c_{j}]]></fr:tex> — never in the journal, introduced when the node was first written.</fr:meta>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>The characteristic polynomial of the generic matrix <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[X]]></fr:tex> of
<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0001/" title="Setup: conjugation over a finite field" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0001/" display-uri="ffi-0001" type="local">the setup</fr:link>: <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  \chi _{X}(\lambda ) = \lambda ^n - c_{1} \lambda ^{n-1}
  + c_{2} \lambda ^{n-2} - \cdots  + (-1)^n c_{n},
  \qquad  c_{j} \in  S^G, \quad  \deg  c_{j} = j.
]]></fr:tex>
So <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c_{1} = \operatorname {tr} X]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c_{n} = \det  X]]></fr:tex>. The signs are
irrelevant to everything downstream.</html:p>
                <html:p><html:strong>Role in the skeleton.</html:strong> These <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n]]></fr:tex> invariants are the entire input to the program:
<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0005/" title="The Steenrod ideal" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0005/" display-uri="ffi-0005" type="local">the Steenrod ideal</fr:link> is generated by their <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[q]]></fr:tex>-Steenrod
powers, and <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0004/" title="Polarizations and the trace-form generators" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0004/" display-uri="ffi-0004" type="local">the polarizations</fr:link> are their bihomogeneous
parts.</html:p>
                <html:p><html:strong>Warning.</html:strong> The <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c_{j}]]></fr:tex> do <html:em>not</html:em> generate <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[S^G]]></fr:tex>. They generate
the invariants of the <html:em>algebraic</html:em> group <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathrm {GL}_n]]></fr:tex> acting by
conjugation; the group here is the finite <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[G = \mathrm {GL}_n(k)]]></fr:tex>, and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[S]]></fr:tex> is
integral over <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[S^G]]></fr:tex>, so <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\dim  S^G = n^2]]></fr:tex> while
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[k[c_{1}, \dots , c_{n}]]]></fr:tex> has dimension <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n]]></fr:tex>. Already at
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n = 2]]></fr:tex>, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[q = 2]]></fr:tex> the pair <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[(c_{1}, c_{2})]]></fr:tex> fails to
separate conjugacy classes: <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[0]]></fr:tex> and
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\begin {pmatrix} 0 & 0 \\ 1 & 0 \end {pmatrix}]]></fr:tex> agree on both. Only
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c_{j} \in  S^G]]></fr:tex> is used anywhere in the program.</html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors />
                <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0055/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>ffi-0055</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/ffi/ffi-0055/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="The entrywise Frobenius">The entrywise Frobenius</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>definition</fr:taxon>
                <fr:meta name="area">foundations</fr:meta>
                <fr:meta name="provenance">distilled.tex Part 1; journal sec:setup.</fr:meta>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>Let <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[K = \overline {\mathbb {F}_q}]]></fr:tex> be the algebraically closed field of
<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0001/" title="Setup: conjugation over a finite field" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0001/" display-uri="ffi-0001" type="local">the setup</fr:link>. For <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[M \in  \mathrm {M}_n(K)]]></fr:tex>, write <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[F(M)]]></fr:tex> for the
<html:em>entrywise Frobenius</html:em> <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[(m_{ij}) \mapsto  (m_{ij}^q)]]></fr:tex>. Then <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[F]]></fr:tex>
is a bijective ring endomorphism of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathrm {M}_n(K)]]></fr:tex> which preserves and
reflects nilpotency, so <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[F(\mathcal {N}) = \mathcal {N}]]></fr:tex>.</html:p>
                <html:p><html:strong>Mechanism.</html:strong> The <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[q]]></fr:tex>-power map is a ring endomorphism of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[K]]></fr:tex>; its
additivity in characteristic <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[p]]></fr:tex> is what makes it compatible with
matrix multiplication, and it is bijective because Frobenius is
surjective on the algebraically closed <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[K]]></fr:tex>. A bijective ring
endomorphism carries nilpotents to nilpotents both ways.</html:p>
                <html:p><html:strong>Role in the skeleton.</html:strong> <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[F]]></fr:tex> is the direction in which the whole program deforms.
<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0003/" title="The evaluation identity" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0003/" display-uri="ffi-0003" type="local">The evaluation identity</fr:link> reads the <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[q]]></fr:tex>-Steenrod operations
off the pencil <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[M + T F(M)]]></fr:tex>, and <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0006/" title="The pencil criterion" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0006/" display-uri="ffi-0006" type="local">the pencil criterion</fr:link>
turns membership in <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[V(J)]]></fr:tex> into nilpotency of every member of
that pencil. It is also the twist that makes <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0011/" title="The trace form" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0011/" display-uri="ffi-0011" type="local">the trace form</fr:link>
identify <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathfrak {gl}_n]]></fr:tex> with its <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[F]]></fr:tex>-twisted dual.</html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors />
                <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0002/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>ffi-0002</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/ffi/ffi-0002/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="The q-Steenrod operations">The q-Steenrod operations</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>definition</fr:taxon>
                <fr:meta name="area">foundations</fr:meta>
                <fr:meta name="provenance">Maithani 2025; L. Smith, <html:em>Polynomial Invariants of Finite Groups</html:em>, Ch.~11.</fr:meta>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>On the coordinate ring <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[S]]></fr:tex> of <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0001/" title="Setup: conjugation over a finite field" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0001/" display-uri="ffi-0001" type="local">the setup</fr:link>, the
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[q]]></fr:tex>-Steenrod operations are the <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[k]]></fr:tex>-algebra homomorphism <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  \mathcal {P} \colon  S \to  S[T], \qquad  x \mapsto  x + x^q T
]]></fr:tex>
on linear forms, with <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[\mathcal {P}(f) = \sum _{i \ge  0} \mathcal {P}^{i}(f) T^i]]></fr:tex>.
Each <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathcal {P}^{i}]]></fr:tex> raises degree by <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[i(q-1)]]></fr:tex> and preserves the
invariants of any degree-preserving action. For homogeneous <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f]]></fr:tex> of
degree <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[d]]></fr:tex> with <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {F}_q]]></fr:tex>-coefficients, <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  \mathcal {P}^{i}(f) = 0 \text { for } i > d,
  \qquad  \mathcal {P}^{d}(f) = f^q.
]]></fr:tex></html:p>
                <html:p><html:strong>Consequence.</html:strong> These two vanishing facts are what make
<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0005/" title="The Steenrod ideal" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0005/" display-uri="ffi-0005" type="local">the Steenrod ideal</fr:link> finitely generated in the stated
degrees.</html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors />
                <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0003/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>ffi-0003</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/ffi/ffi-0003/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="The evaluation identity">The evaluation identity</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>theorem</fr:taxon>
                <fr:meta name="area">foundations</fr:meta>
                <fr:meta name="status">proven</fr:meta>
                <fr:meta name="provenance">distilled.tex Part 1; journal sec:steenrod.</fr:meta>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>Let <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[S]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[K]]></fr:tex> be as in <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0001/" title="Setup: conjugation over a finite field" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0001/" display-uri="ffi-0001" type="local">the setup</fr:link>, let <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathcal {P}^{i}]]></fr:tex> be
the <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0002/" title="The q-Steenrod operations" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0002/" display-uri="ffi-0002" type="local"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[q]]></fr:tex>-Steenrod operations</fr:link> and let <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[F]]></fr:tex> be the
<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0055/" title="The entrywise Frobenius" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0055/" display-uri="ffi-0055" type="local">entrywise Frobenius</fr:link>. For every <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[f \in  S]]></fr:tex>
and every <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[M \in  \mathrm {M}_n(K)]]></fr:tex>, <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  \sum _{i \ge  0} \mathcal {P}^{i}(f)(M) T^i = f(M + T F(M))
  \qquad  \text {in } K[T].
]]></fr:tex></html:p>
                <html:p><html:strong>Mechanism.</html:strong> <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathcal {P}]]></fr:tex> is a substitution homomorphism, so it commutes with
evaluation. This one line is what the whole height program runs on:
<html:em>Steenrod operations are deformation calculus in the Frobenius
direction</html:em>. Every subsequent statement about <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[V(J)]]></fr:tex> — the
pencil criterion, the flag witnesses — is read off it.</html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors />
                <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0004/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>ffi-0004</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/ffi/ffi-0004/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="Polarizations and the trace-form generators">Polarizations and the trace-form generators</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>definition</fr:taxon>
                <fr:meta name="area">foundations</fr:meta>
                <fr:meta name="provenance">journal sec:polarization.</fr:meta>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>For commuting indeterminates <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[s, t]]></fr:tex> and two generic matrices
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[X, Y]]></fr:tex>, the <html:em>polarizations</html:em> <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[P_{a,b}]]></fr:tex> of
<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0054/" title="The characteristic polynomial coefficients" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0054/" display-uri="ffi-0054" type="local">the characteristic polynomial coefficients</fr:link> <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[c_{j}]]></fr:tex>
are defined by <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  c_{j}(sX + tY) = \sum _{a + b = j} s^a t^b P_{a,b}(X, Y),
]]></fr:tex>
bihomogeneous of bidegree <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[(a, b)]]></fr:tex>, with
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[P_{j,0} = c_{j}(X)]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[P_{0,j} = c_{j}(Y)]]></fr:tex>.</html:p>
                <html:p>For <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[j \le  4]]></fr:tex> these are explicit combinations of trace forms
twisted by <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0055/" title="The entrywise Frobenius" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0055/" display-uri="ffi-0055" type="local">the entrywise Frobenius</fr:link>, <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  \operatorname {tr}(X^{a_1} F(X)^{b_1} \cdots  X^{a_r} F(X)^{b_r})
]]></fr:tex>
— the sparse trace-form generators. Setting <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[Y = F(X)]]></fr:tex> recovers the
<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0002/" title="The q-Steenrod operations" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0002/" display-uri="ffi-0002" type="local"><fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[q]]></fr:tex>-Steenrod powers</fr:link>, by
<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0003/" title="The evaluation identity" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0003/" display-uri="ffi-0003" type="local">the evaluation identity</fr:link>.</html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors />
                <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0005/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>ffi-0005</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/ffi/ffi-0005/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="The Steenrod ideal">The Steenrod ideal</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>definition</fr:taxon>
                <fr:meta name="area">foundations</fr:meta>
                <fr:meta name="provenance">Maithani 2025 for <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n = 2]]></fr:tex>; journal sec:ideal.</fr:meta>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p><fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  J \coloneqq  \bigl ( \mathcal {P}^{i}(c_{j}) : 1 \le  j \le  n,
  0 \le  i \le  j-1 \bigr ) \subseteq  S,
]]></fr:tex>
the ideal generated by the <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[q]]></fr:tex>-Steenrod powers of
<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0054/" title="The characteristic polynomial coefficients" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0054/" display-uri="ffi-0054" type="local">the characteristic polynomial coefficients</fr:link>: a family of
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\binom {n+1}{2} = \frac {n(n+1)}{2}]]></fr:tex> invariants, of degrees <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[j + i(q-1)]]></fr:tex>.</html:p>
                <html:p>By the vanishing facts of <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0002/" title="The q-Steenrod operations" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0002/" display-uri="ffi-0002" type="local">the <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[q]]></fr:tex>-Steenrod operations</fr:link>
the omitted Steenrod powers contribute nothing, so <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[V(J)]]></fr:tex> is
the common zero locus of <html:em>all</html:em> <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathcal {P}^{i}(c_{j})]]></fr:tex>,
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[i \ge  0]]></fr:tex>. For <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n = 2]]></fr:tex> the ideal is exactly Maithani's primary ideal
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[(\operatorname {tr}, \det , \mathcal {P}^{1}(\det ))]]></fr:tex> up to the orbit product.</html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors />
                <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0006/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>ffi-0006</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/ffi/ffi-0006/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="The pencil criterion">The pencil criterion</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>theorem</fr:taxon>
                <fr:meta name="area">foundations</fr:meta>
                <fr:meta name="status">proven</fr:meta>
                <fr:meta name="provenance">distilled.tex Part 1; journal sec:pencil.</fr:meta>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>Let <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[J]]></fr:tex> be <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0005/" title="The Steenrod ideal" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0005/" display-uri="ffi-0005" type="local">the Steenrod ideal</fr:link>. For
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[M \in  \mathrm {M}_n(K)]]></fr:tex>: <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  M \in  V(J) \iff  M + t F(M)
  \text { is nilpotent for every } t \in  K.
]]></fr:tex>
Equivalently <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  M \in  V(J) \iff 
  \operatorname {span}_K \lbrace  M, F(M) \rbrace  \subseteq  \mathcal {N} :
]]></fr:tex>
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[V(J)]]></fr:tex> is the locus of matrices <html:em>nilpotent to all orders
in the Frobenius direction</html:em>. In particular
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[F(V(J)) = V(J)]]></fr:tex>.</html:p>
                <html:p><html:strong>Mechanism.</html:strong> Immediate from <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0003/" title="The evaluation identity" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0003/" display-uri="ffi-0003" type="local">the evaluation identity</fr:link>: the
coefficients of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\chi _{M + tF(M)}]]></fr:tex> are the
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathcal {P}^{i}(c_{j})(M)]]></fr:tex>, so all of them vanish exactly when every
member of the pencil is nilpotent.</html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors />
                <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0007/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>ffi-0007</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/ffi/ffi-0007/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="Flag witnesses for the partner variety">Flag witnesses for the partner variety</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>proposition</fr:taxon>
                <fr:meta name="area">foundations</fr:meta>
                <fr:meta name="status">proven</fr:meta>
                <fr:meta name="provenance">journal sec:notflag (the <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n = 3]]></fr:tex> non-flag component is a cone over a twisted cubic); distilled.tex Part 1.</fr:meta>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>Let <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathfrak {n}]]></fr:tex> be the strictly upper triangular matrices. Then <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  \mathcal {FL} \coloneqq  \bigcup _{g \in  \mathrm {GL}_n(\mathbb {F}_q)} g \mathfrak {n} g^{-1}
  \subseteq  V(J),
]]></fr:tex>
a finite union of linear subspaces of dimension <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\frac {n(n - 1)}{2}]]></fr:tex>, indexed
by the <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {F}_q]]></fr:tex>-rational complete flags.</html:p>
                <html:p>More generally, by <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0006/" title="The pencil criterion" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0006/" display-uri="ffi-0006" type="local">the pencil criterion</fr:link>, any nilpotent
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[M]]></fr:tex> with <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[[M, F(M)] = 0]]></fr:tex> lies in <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[V(J)]]></fr:tex>, as does any
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[M]]></fr:tex> such that <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[M]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[F(M)]]></fr:tex> are strictly triangular with respect
to a common flag. For <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n = 2]]></fr:tex> these mechanisms exhaust
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[V(J)]]></fr:tex>; already for <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n = 3]]></fr:tex> they do not, so the flag locus
is not all of the partner variety — only, conjecturally, all of it up to
dimension.</html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors />
                <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0008/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>ffi-0008</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/ffi/ffi-0008/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="The Krull bound: only the upper bound is open">The Krull bound: only the upper bound is open</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>proposition</fr:taxon>
                <fr:meta name="area">foundations</fr:meta>
                <fr:meta name="status">proven</fr:meta>
                <fr:meta name="provenance">journal sec:ideal.</fr:meta>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>Krull's height theorem, applied to the <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\frac {n(n+1)}{2}]]></fr:tex> generators of
<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0005/" title="The Steenrod ideal" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0005/" display-uri="ffi-0005" type="local">the Steenrod ideal</fr:link>, gives <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\operatorname {ht} J \le  \frac {n(n+1)}{2}]]></fr:tex>
unconditionally, that is <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\dim  V(J) \ge  \frac {n(n - 1)}{2}]]></fr:tex>, and
<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0007/" title="Flag witnesses for the partner variety" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0007/" display-uri="ffi-0007" type="local">the flag witnesses</fr:link> attain that lower bound.</html:p>
                <html:p><html:strong>Consequence.</html:strong> The whole content of <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0009/" title="The height conjecture" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0009/" display-uri="ffi-0009" type="local">the height conjecture</fr:link> is
therefore an <html:em>upper</html:em> bound on <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\dim  V(J)]]></fr:tex>. The generators
of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[J]]></fr:tex> extend to a homogeneous system of parameters if and only if the conjectured height
holds.</html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors />
                <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0009/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>ffi-0009</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/ffi/ffi-0009/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="The height conjecture">The height conjecture</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>conjecture</fr:taxon>
                <fr:meta name="area">foundations</fr:meta>
                <fr:meta name="area">height</fr:meta>
                <fr:meta name="status">open</fr:meta>
                <fr:meta name="provenance">distilled.tex Part 1; journal thm:height-n7, thm:m8, cor:height-n8, cor:height-n9.</fr:meta>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>Let <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[J]]></fr:tex> be <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0005/" title="The Steenrod ideal" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0005/" display-uri="ffi-0005" type="local">the Steenrod ideal</fr:link>. For every <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n]]></fr:tex>
and every prime power <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[q]]></fr:tex>, <fr:tex display="block"><![CDATA[
  \operatorname {ht} J = \frac {n(n+1)}{2},
  \qquad  \text {equivalently} \qquad 
  \dim  V(J) = \frac {n(n - 1)}{2}:
]]></fr:tex>
the <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\frac {n(n+1)}{2}]]></fr:tex> invariants <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathcal {P}^{i}(c_{j})]]></fr:tex>,
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[0 \le  i < j \le  n]]></fr:tex>, form a regular sequence in <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[S]]></fr:tex> and extend to a
homogeneous system of parameters. The conjectured dimension is <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\dim  \mathrm {Fl}]]></fr:tex> — up to dimension, the
flag witnesses must be everything. By <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0008/" title="The Krull bound: only the upper bound is open" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0008/" display-uri="ffi-0008" type="local">the Krull bound</fr:link> the
inequality <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\ge ]]></fr:tex> is free, so the whole content is the reverse one.</html:p>
                <html:p><html:strong>Status.</html:strong> Proven for <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n \le  5]]></fr:tex> every <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[q]]></fr:tex>
(<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0019/" title="Height at n ≤ 5" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0019/" display-uri="ffi-0019" type="local">height at <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n \le  5]]></fr:tex></fr:link>); for characteristic <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[0]]></fr:tex>, every <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n]]></fr:tex>;
for all but finitely many characteristics per <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n]]></fr:tex>, ineffectively; and
for <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n \le  9]]></fr:tex> every <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[q]]></fr:tex> (<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0018/" title="Height at n ≤ 9" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0018/" display-uri="ffi-0018" type="local">height at <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n \le  9]]></fr:tex></fr:link>). Open in
general, and what is open is exactly <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0010/" title="(*): the characteristic-p subvariety inference" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0010/" display-uri="ffi-0010" type="local">(*)</fr:link>.</html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors />
                <fr:uri>https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0010/</fr:uri>
                <fr:display-uri>ffi-0010</fr:display-uri>
                <fr:route>/ffi/ffi-0010/</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="(*): the characteristic-p subvariety inference">(*): the characteristic-p subvariety inference</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>conjecture</fr:taxon>
                <fr:meta name="area">foundations</fr:meta>
                <fr:meta name="area">height</fr:meta>
                <fr:meta name="area">frontier</fr:meta>
                <fr:meta name="status">open</fr:meta>
                <fr:meta name="provenance">distilled.tex Part 3; journal sec:h1 and the pass 6--8 apparatus.</fr:meta>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <html:p>(*): for every proper <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[B]]></fr:tex>-stable irreducible subvariety
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[W]]></fr:tex> of a nilpotent orbit <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathcal {O}_{\lambda }]]></fr:tex>, the isotropy bound holds —
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\dim  W \le  \frac {1}{2} \dim  \mathcal {O}_{\lambda }]]></fr:tex>, with <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\dim  \mathcal {O}_{\lambda }]]></fr:tex> the
orbit dimension of <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0012/" title="The three recurring numbers" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0012/" display-uri="ffi-0012" type="local">the three recurring numbers</fr:link>.</html:p>
                <html:p><html:strong>Role in the skeleton.</html:strong> After <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0016/" title="(H1): the incidence theorem for flagless pencils" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0016/" display-uri="ffi-0016" type="local">(H1)</fr:link> this is the single remaining geometric
statement between the program and <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0009/" title="The height conjecture" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0009/" display-uri="ffi-0009" type="local">the height conjecture</fr:link>.
<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0014/" title="Orbital varieties are Lagrangian" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0014/" display-uri="ffi-0014" type="local">Orbital varieties are Lagrangian</fr:link> supplies it whenever <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[W]]></fr:tex>
is an orbital variety; what is left is the case of subvarieties carrying
<fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\ge  2]]></fr:tex> moduli of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[B]]></fr:tex>-orbits, where characteristic <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[p]]></fr:tex> denies
the generic-smoothness argument. First possible at <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n = 6]]></fr:tex>, which is
why the ladder <fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0018/" title="Height at n ≤ 9" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0018/" display-uri="ffi-0018" type="local">height at <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[n \le  9]]></fr:tex></fr:link> has to be climbed by
hand. The infinitesimal form of the same statement is
<fr:link href="/ffi/ffi-0050/" title="(W): the wall conjecture" uri="https://www.brendan.phd/ffi/ffi-0050/" display-uri="ffi-0050" type="local">(W)</fr:link>.</html:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
      </fr:mainmatter>
    </fr:tree>
